  <data name="History.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Our history | The Rachel Tower</value></data>
  <data name="History.MetaDescription" xml:space="preserve"><value>How The Rachel Tower was built, from buying the land in 2020 to opening in August 2023.</value></data>
  <data name="History.Eyebrow" xml:space="preserve"><value>Since 2020</value></data>
  <data name="History.Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>A glimpse of our history</value></data>
  <data name="History.Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>Since 2020, The Rachel Tower has grown from a dream into reality. Here are the key moments of its construction, followed by some of the drawings made while the building was being planned.</value></data>
  <data name="History.GalleryTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plans and construction</value></data>
  <data name="History.PhotoAlt" xml:space="preserve"><value>Planning drawing or construction photo {0}</value></data>
  <data name="History.1.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>2 March 2020</value></data>
  <data name="History.1.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Buying the land</value></data>
  <data name="History.1.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>The empty plot was bought from a lawyer in Kolwezi, Lualaba. The deal was concluded on the evening of 2 March 2020.</value></data>
  <data name="History.2.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>6 April 2020</value></data>
  <data name="History.2.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Clearing the site</value></data>
  <data name="History.2.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>A month later, the land was cleared with a backhoe loader (TLB) rented from La Générale de Forage, under the supervision of Norbert Mahuwa.</value></data>
  <data name="History.3.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>Before construction</value></data>
  <data name="History.3.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Digging the borehole</value></data>
  <data name="History.3.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>Because water is hard to come by in the area, an 85-metre borehole was drilled with a final casing of 125 mm. Water was reached at 43 metres, and an electric submersible pump was installed at 55 metres.</value></data>
  <data name="History.4.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>10 April 2021</value></data>
  <data name="History.4.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Laying the foundations</value></data>
  <data name="History.4.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>Work on the foundations began. 24 pillars were set 2.20 metres deep on one-square-metre concrete pads reinforced with 12 mm steel. Reinforced ground beams were built under every future wall and topped with large stones supplied by Forrest Kolwezi. The ground was backfilled with laterite and compacted with a machine rented from EKMM SARL.</value></data>
  <data name="History.5.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>22 March 2022</value></data>
  <data name="History.5.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Raising the walls</value></data>
  <data name="History.5.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>After a pause of a year, construction resumed. The walls were built with 20 × 20 × 40 cm bricks supplied by Kilma Bricks in Musompo, Kolwezi.</value></data>
  <data name="History.6.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>15 August 2022</value></data>
  <data name="History.6.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ground floor complete</value></data>
  <data name="History.6.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>The ground floor was finished, and the beams and formwork for the concrete slab were nearly ready.</value></data>
  <data name="History.7.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>27 August 2022</value></data>
  <data name="History.7.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pouring the slab</value></data>
  <data name="History.7.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>Forty people worked for 24 hours straight to pour the 196 m² slab: 300 bags (7.5 tonnes) of cement mixed with crushed sand from Katonto, poured and vibrated across the whole surface. It was the most expensive stage of the entire build.</value></data>
  <data name="History.8.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>9 December 2022</value></data>
  <data name="History.8.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The upper floor</value></data>
  <data name="History.8.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>The first and final floor was completed by engineer François Cedrick Banza, to a design by architect Joseph Kasiho Biamungu.</value></data>
  <data name="History.9.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>31 Dec. 2022 to 11 Jan. 2023</value></data>
  <data name="History.9.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The roof frame</value></data>
  <data name="History.9.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>A team of three led by Mambe designed and built the fir-wood frame that supports the roof.</value></data>
  <data name="History.10.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>11 to 20 January 2023</value></data>
  <data name="History.10.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Finishing touches</value></data>
  <data name="History.10.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>The roof tiles, imported from China, went on in ten days: 566 red tiles and 34 ridge and valley trays.</value></data>
  <data name="History.11.Date" xml:space="preserve"><value>28 August 2023</value></data>
  <data name="History.11.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Opening day</value></data>
  <data name="History.11.Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Rachel Tower opened its doors and welcomed its first guests.</value></data>
